1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,300
Listen. Practice. Speak. Practice makes you perfect.

2
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:21,300
Welcome to our Business English Learning Dialogue on Essential Skills for Future Business Professionals.

3
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:28,360
Today, we're focusing on how Generative AI is transforming the workplace.

4
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:35,360
Have you noticed how these AI tools are becoming a critical skill for almost every professional?

5
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:36,980
Absolutely.

6
00:00:36,980 --> 00:00:44,740
The modern workplace is evolving so fast that understanding generative AI is no longer optional.

7
00:00:44,740 --> 00:00:50,500
It's becoming a core competency, just like communication or problem solving.

8
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:51,620
True.

9
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:57,420
One of the biggest advantages is how AI helps professionals work smarter.

10
00:00:57,420 --> 00:01:03,700
Tasks like research, summarizing reports, drafting emails, or analyzing trends can

11
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:05,420
now be done in minutes.

12
00:01:05,420 --> 00:01:06,700
Exactly.

13
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:10,420
And that means professionals need to master AI collaboration

14
00:01:10,420 --> 00:01:14,380
skills, not just knowing what AI can do,

15
00:01:14,380 --> 00:01:17,210
but understanding how to direct it.

16
00:01:17,210 --> 00:01:21,690
Prompting, reviewing outputs, and applying critical thinking

17
00:01:21,690 --> 00:01:23,490
are essential skills now.

18
00:01:23,490 --> 00:01:25,410
Critical thinking is key.

19
00:01:25,410 --> 00:01:27,810
Without it, there's a risk of accepting

20
00:01:27,810 --> 00:01:32,330
AI generated information without verifying accuracy.

21
00:01:32,330 --> 00:01:35,990
Professionals most learn to evaluate, cross-check,

22
00:01:35,990 --> 00:01:38,570
and refine AI results.

23
00:01:38,570 --> 00:01:41,970
Another important skill is data literacy.

24
00:01:41,970 --> 00:01:45,090
Even if someone isn't in a technical role,

25
00:01:45,090 --> 00:01:48,210
they need to understand how data works

26
00:01:48,210 --> 00:01:52,170
because AI relies heavily on good, clean data.

27
00:01:52,170 --> 00:01:55,470
Absolutely, and understanding AI ethics

28
00:01:55,470 --> 00:01:57,450
is becoming crucial too.

29
00:01:57,450 --> 00:02:02,010
Issues like bias, transparency, and responsible usage

30
00:02:02,010 --> 00:02:04,650
are now part of business decision making.

31
00:02:04,650 --> 00:02:05,990
You're right.

32
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:10,210
Organizations want employees who can use AI responsibly

33
00:02:10,210 --> 00:02:14,730
while keeping privacy, compliance, and fairness in mind.

34
00:02:14,730 --> 00:02:17,970
One more future skill is adaptability.

35
00:02:17,970 --> 00:02:20,770
Since AI tools are evolving rapidly,

36
00:02:20,770 --> 00:02:22,530
professionals must be comfortable

37
00:02:22,530 --> 00:02:24,730
learning new technologies on the go.

38
00:02:24,730 --> 00:02:25,690
I agree.

39
00:02:25,690 --> 00:02:27,450
The most successful professionals

40
00:02:27,450 --> 00:02:31,650
will be those who embrace change instead of resisting it.

41
00:02:31,650 --> 00:02:35,450
Have you noticed how AI is also reshaping teamwork?

42
00:02:35,450 --> 00:02:38,810
Collaboration now means working not only with colleagues,

43
00:02:38,810 --> 00:02:41,130
but also with AI systems.

44
00:02:41,130 --> 00:02:42,610
Exactly.

45
00:02:42,610 --> 00:02:45,810
And tools like AI-powered project planners,

46
00:02:45,810 --> 00:02:47,610
brainstorming assistants,

47
00:02:47,610 --> 00:02:49,970
and real-time analytics dashboards

48
00:02:49,970 --> 00:02:52,570
are enhancing team performance.

49
00:02:52,570 --> 00:02:55,810
And communication skills remain essential,

50
00:02:55,810 --> 00:02:58,090
especially when professionals need to present

51
00:02:58,090 --> 00:03:03,090
AI-generated insights clearly and confidently in meetings.

52
00:03:03,090 --> 00:03:09,810
Presentation skills, storytelling skills, and persuasive speaking are becoming even more

53
00:03:09,810 --> 00:03:11,310
important.

54
00:03:11,310 --> 00:03:17,210
AI can provide the data, but humans must communicate it effectively.

55
00:03:17,210 --> 00:03:20,610
Do you think all professionals are ready for this shift?

56
00:03:20,610 --> 00:03:21,610
Not yet.

57
00:03:21,610 --> 00:03:25,430
That's why upskilling is so crucial.

58
00:03:25,430 --> 00:03:32,790
programs, workshops, and hands-on practice with AI tools will prepare teams for the future.

59
00:03:32,790 --> 00:03:39,110
I think organizations should consider launching a company-wide program to help employees develop

60
00:03:39,110 --> 00:03:45,430
these essential AI skills. That would be a great idea. We could even bring in experts to

61
00:03:45,430 --> 00:03:52,550
demonstrate real use cases and help employees understand how generative AI supports productivity.

62
00:03:52,550 --> 00:04:00,550
Let's draft a proposal for leadership. With the right training, our teams will be well equipped for the future of business.

63
00:04:00,550 --> 00:04:08,550
Agreed. Let's make sure our organization leads the way in embracing AI confidently and responsibly.

64
00:04:08,550 --> 00:04:15,650
Have you noticed how generative AI is becoming a core skill for future business professionals?

65
00:04:15,650 --> 00:04:24,010
Absolutely. Understanding how to use AI tools effectively is now just as important as traditional

66
00:04:24,010 --> 00:04:26,200
workplace skills.

67
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:33,120
AI accelerates research, reporting, content creation, and data analysis. Professionals

68
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:36,380
need to learn how to collaborate with AI.

69
00:04:36,380 --> 00:04:43,430
And that means being good at prompting, reviewing outputs, and applying critical thinking.

70
00:04:43,430 --> 00:04:51,030
Social thinking helps avoid mistakes and ensures AI-generated information is accurate and reliable.

71
00:04:51,030 --> 00:04:53,590
Data literacy is another essential skill.

72
00:04:53,590 --> 00:05:00,310
AI depends on good data, so professionals need to understand how data is collected,

73
00:05:00,310 --> 00:05:03,130
used, and interpreted.

74
00:05:03,130 --> 00:05:05,930
Ethical awareness is also important.

75
00:05:05,930 --> 00:05:12,690
Professionals must consider bias, transparency, and privacy when using AI tools.

76
00:05:12,690 --> 00:05:19,090
is becoming a top priority too. AI tools evolve quickly, so employees must be open

77
00:05:19,090 --> 00:05:21,470
to continuous learning.

78
00:05:21,470 --> 00:05:28,790
Collaboration now includes working with AI-powered systems to improve performance and productivity.

79
00:05:28,790 --> 00:05:35,070
And communication skills are more important than ever. Professionals must explain AI-driven

80
00:05:35,070 --> 00:05:39,050
insights clearly and persuasively.

81
00:05:39,050 --> 00:05:41,790
skilling programs can help employees develop

82
00:05:41,790 --> 00:05:44,090
these future ready capabilities.

83
00:05:44,090 --> 00:05:45,050
Agreed.

84
00:05:45,050 --> 00:05:47,470
With proper training, organizations

85
00:05:47,470 --> 00:05:51,560
can unlock the full potential of generative AI.

86
00:05:51,560 --> 00:05:54,160
Let's shift to production capacity.

87
00:05:54,160 --> 00:05:56,120
Where are we struggling the most?

88
00:05:56,120 --> 00:05:57,960
Mainly in Plant 2.

89
00:05:57,960 --> 00:06:01,640
The machinery is outdated and downtime has increased.

90
00:06:01,640 --> 00:06:03,560
We're spending more on repairs

91
00:06:03,560 --> 00:06:06,640
than replacements would cost in the long run.

92
00:06:06,640 --> 00:06:10,560
Also workforce fatigue is becoming a problem.

93
00:06:10,560 --> 00:06:14,080
Output has dropped not because people are lazy

94
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:16,400
but because they're stretched.

95
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:19,320
Some employees have already expressed concerns

96
00:06:19,320 --> 00:06:22,400
about workload and unclear direction.

97
00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:26,640
Then we need to address both technology and morale.

98
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:29,760
For technology, we can propose equipment upgrades.

99
00:06:29,760 --> 00:06:34,040
For morale, maybe training, clear communication

100
00:06:34,040 --> 00:06:36,680
and better workload distribution.

101
00:06:36,680 --> 00:06:38,000
Agreed.

102
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,240
And maybe include appreciation initiatives.

103
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:44,560
Even simple recognition can go a long way.

104
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:45,400
True.

105
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:48,520
People want to feel seen, not just used.

106
00:06:48,520 --> 00:06:51,520
Deep dive financial structure.

107
00:06:51,520 --> 00:06:52,800
Let's talk money.

108
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:55,880
If we want plant upgrades, supply chain upgrades,

109
00:06:55,880 --> 00:06:59,430
or additional staff, we need to justify the budget.

110
00:06:59,430 --> 00:07:02,270
Have you estimated the financial implications?

111
00:07:02,270 --> 00:07:04,310
Roughly, yes.

112
00:07:04,310 --> 00:07:06,510
The equipment upgrade for plant two

113
00:07:06,510 --> 00:07:10,600
might cost around 2.7 million,

114
00:07:10,600 --> 00:07:12,540
but the payoff is strong,

115
00:07:12,540 --> 00:07:15,500
higher efficiency, lower maintenance costs,

116
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:17,540
and fewer delays.

117
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:19,380
What's the payback period?

118
00:07:19,380 --> 00:07:22,380
Estimated 18 to 20 months.

119
00:07:22,380 --> 00:07:23,780
That's acceptable,

120
00:07:23,780 --> 00:07:26,960
especially with the growth targets we have.

121
00:07:26,960 --> 00:07:29,640
Let's imagine the company one year from now,

122
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:35,960
What needs to be true for us to hit the board's growth targets without collapsing internally?

123
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:42,360
A few things. Supply chain reliability must improve by at least 30 percent.

124
00:07:43,280 --> 00:07:49,800
Production capacity must increase by 15 to 20 percent. Morale must rise.

125
00:07:50,760 --> 00:07:57,880
Processes must become more automated. Sales and marketing must align more closely with

126
00:07:57,880 --> 00:08:00,930
actual operational capabilities,

127
00:08:00,930 --> 00:08:04,410
internal communication must become seamless.

128
00:08:04,410 --> 00:08:07,250
Exactly, and that means our strategy

129
00:08:07,250 --> 00:08:09,850
should focus on operational excellence,

130
00:08:09,850 --> 00:08:13,650
customer satisfaction, and scalable growth.

131
00:08:13,650 --> 00:08:14,730
I agree.

132
00:08:14,730 --> 00:08:17,210
If we strengthen the internal foundation,

133
00:08:17,210 --> 00:08:20,740
growth becomes a natural byproduct.

134
00:08:20,740 --> 00:08:23,940
HR plus talent development section.

135
00:08:23,940 --> 00:08:25,660
Let's talk about people.

136
00:08:25,660 --> 00:08:30,220
We've been hearing concerns that mid-level managers feel overwhelmed.

137
00:08:30,220 --> 00:08:31,460
Yes.

138
00:08:31,460 --> 00:08:34,620
They're caught between pressure from senior leadership

139
00:08:34,620 --> 00:08:37,580
and expectations from the workforce.

140
00:08:37,580 --> 00:08:44,220
They need support, training, clearer KPIs, and communication tools.

141
00:08:44,220 --> 00:08:46,820
Are we at risk of losing key people?

142
00:08:46,820 --> 00:08:48,220
Potentially.

143
00:08:48,220 --> 00:08:52,260
Two team leads have hinted they're considering other opportunities.

144
00:08:52,260 --> 00:08:55,860
Their main issues are workload and lack of clarity

145
00:08:55,860 --> 00:08:57,860
about the company's direction.

146
00:08:57,860 --> 00:09:02,420
Then we need to intervene before it becomes a resignation.

147
00:09:02,420 --> 00:09:05,460
Conversation goes deep into talent mapping,

148
00:09:05,460 --> 00:09:08,460
succession planning, conflict resolution,

149
00:09:08,460 --> 00:09:11,500
leadership training, employee engagement,

150
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:15,540
performance review systems, and wellness programs.

151
00:09:15,540 --> 00:09:18,760
Crisis management scenario, by the way,

152
00:09:18,760 --> 00:09:20,740
did you hear about the customer complaint

153
00:09:20,740 --> 00:09:22,220
escalation last week?

154
00:09:22,220 --> 00:09:29,220
Yes. They experienced a major delay due to missing documents. That shouldn't have happened.

155
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:33,460
One department assumed another department handled the paperwork.

156
00:09:33,460 --> 00:09:35,460
Classic coordination failure.

157
00:09:35,460 --> 00:09:39,740
Exactly. And the customer was furious.

158
00:09:39,740 --> 00:09:45,780
We managed to calm them down after apologizing, but we can't let such incidents repeat.

159
00:09:45,780 --> 00:09:51,100
What about negotiations with the logistics provider? Are we close to securing better

160
00:09:51,100 --> 00:09:52,100
terms?

161
00:09:52,100 --> 00:09:53,650
We're getting there.

162
00:09:53,650 --> 00:09:59,410
They proposed a revised contract with a 4% rate increase, but I think we can negotiate

163
00:09:59,410 --> 00:10:04,490
it down to 2% if we offer a longer-term partnership.

164
00:10:04,490 --> 00:10:05,690
Then go for it.

165
00:10:05,690 --> 00:10:09,660
Emphasize stability and volume commitment.

166
00:10:09,660 --> 00:10:14,260
This has been one of the most productive discussions we've had in months.

167
00:10:14,260 --> 00:10:16,300
We've touched on every critical area.

168
00:10:16,300 --> 00:10:19,380
Now, let's outline our next steps clearly.

169
00:10:19,380 --> 00:10:20,540
Agreed.

170
00:10:20,540 --> 00:10:25,200
Let me summarize what we've covered and confirm the next actions.

171
00:10:25,200 --> 00:10:26,760
Please go ahead.

172
00:10:26,760 --> 00:10:33,310
First, we will diversify suppliers and initiate onboarding for a secondary vendor.

173
00:10:33,310 --> 00:10:39,400
Second, we will create a weekly forecasting update process with the sales team.

174
00:10:39,400 --> 00:10:46,320
Third, we will establish a real-time communication channel for supply chain coordination.

175
00:10:46,320 --> 00:10:52,350
Fourth, we will prepare a proposal for upgrading equipment at Plant 2.

176
00:10:52,350 --> 00:10:59,290
Fifth, we will introduce initiatives to boost team morale and support middle-level managers.

177
00:10:59,290 --> 00:11:05,120
Sixth, we will improve crisis management protocols and document flow.

178
00:11:05,120 --> 00:11:09,120
Seventh, we will strengthen our negotiation strategy.

179
00:11:09,120 --> 00:11:13,280
Before we get into the numbers, let me ask you something.

180
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:19,920
Over the past month, have you felt that the organization is becoming harder to steer?

181
00:11:19,920 --> 00:11:26,880
I mean, in terms of alignment, priorities, and execution, because from where I'm sitting,

182
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:30,560
things feel like they're drifting slightly out of sync.

183
00:11:31,120 --> 00:11:36,240
You're not imagining it. I've sensed the same thing. People are busy, yes,

184
00:11:36,240 --> 00:11:42,080
but not always aligned. Workloads are high, communication is inconsistent,

185
00:11:42,080 --> 00:11:45,280
and decision-making seems slower than usual.

186
00:11:45,280 --> 00:11:49,600
It's not a crisis yet, but it's trending in a concerning direction.

187
00:11:49,600 --> 00:11:55,360
Exactly. And if we don't address it now, it could become a major problem.

188
00:11:56,080 --> 00:12:01,200
That's why I want this conversation to be as detailed and candid as possible.

189
00:12:01,920 --> 00:12:05,040
We need clarity, structure, and direction.

190
00:12:05,840 --> 00:12:10,720
So let me start by asking, what's your honest assessment of the last quarter?

191
00:12:10,720 --> 00:12:18,160
Honestly? Mixed. We delivered results, but not efficiently. We hit targets, but not confidently.

192
00:12:18,160 --> 00:12:24,960
We responded to issues, but didn't anticipate them. And I think everyone feels the strain.

193
00:12:24,960 --> 00:12:28,880
Performance was acceptable, but the process was messy.

194
00:12:28,880 --> 00:12:35,920
I completely agree. And that tells me the foundation is weakening. Not collapsing,

195
00:12:35,920 --> 00:12:44,320
but weakening. And before we grow or expand or introduce anything new, we need to reinforce

196
00:12:44,320 --> 00:12:50,320
that foundation. Absolutely. So, where do you want to begin? There are so many layers,

197
00:12:50,320 --> 00:12:55,560
supply chain, production alignment, communication gaps, leadership bandwidth.

198
00:12:55,560 --> 00:13:02,320
Let's start with the core operations. Because if the engine doesn't run smoothly,

199
00:13:02,320 --> 00:13:08,600
nothing else works. Understood. So the biggest operational challenge right now

200
00:13:08,600 --> 00:13:15,360
is predictability. We can produce but inconsistently. We can deliver but with

201
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:20,680
fluctuating timelines. And the reason is simple. The planning inputs are

202
00:13:20,680 --> 00:13:27,400
unstable. Sales forecasts change too often and without enough justification.

203
00:13:27,400 --> 00:13:34,320
That's a recurring issue. Sales is optimistic by nature, which is good for morale, but bad

204
00:13:34,320 --> 00:13:40,520
for planning. So, what's your suggestion? How do we anchor the forecasting process so

205
00:13:40,520 --> 00:13:43,160
you can plan with confidence?

206
00:13:43,160 --> 00:13:50,000
Three things. First, we need data-driven forecasting, not optimism-driven forecasting.

207
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:57,040
Second, we need a rolling weekly adjustment window rather than abrupt monthly changes.

208
00:13:57,040 --> 00:13:59,560
Third, we need accountability.

209
00:13:59,560 --> 00:14:05,400
If forecasts deviate wildly from reality, someone must explain why.

210
00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:07,160
I like all three.

211
00:14:07,160 --> 00:14:09,360
The third one especially.

212
00:14:09,360 --> 00:14:14,000
People need to justify their numbers, not just submit them.

213
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:15,960
Accountability creates discipline.

214
00:14:15,960 --> 00:14:17,320
Exactly.

215
00:14:17,320 --> 00:14:21,760
And discipline is the difference between consistency and chaos.

216
00:14:21,760 --> 00:14:25,000
Alright, let's talk about capacity.

217
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:34,080
quarter, you mentioned that plant 2 is operating at only about 76% effective capacity. Why?

218
00:14:34,080 --> 00:14:36,240
What's dragging it down?

219
00:14:36,240 --> 00:14:43,040
Maintenance. Or rather, insufficient maintenance. The machines are aging. Repairs are reactive,

220
00:14:43,040 --> 00:14:49,400
not preventative. So, we face unexpected downtime, and when machines stop unexpectedly,

221
00:14:49,400 --> 00:14:53,560
We lose not just time, but also workforce rhythm.

222
00:14:53,560 --> 00:14:57,960
People get frustrated, morale dips, and output drops.

223
00:14:57,960 --> 00:15:00,200
What's the real solution?

224
00:15:00,200 --> 00:15:03,520
Not a band-aid, an actual fix.

225
00:15:03,520 --> 00:15:10,120
Realistically, a capital investment into new machinery, or at least major upgrades.

226
00:15:10,120 --> 00:15:11,840
Anything else is temporary.

227
00:15:11,840 --> 00:15:15,360
We've stretched the existing equipment as far as it can go.

228
00:15:15,360 --> 00:15:18,160
How much would an upgrade cost?

229
00:15:18,160 --> 00:15:24,320
on the configuration, somewhere between 2.5 to 3 million.

230
00:15:24,320 --> 00:15:25,760
That's significant.

231
00:15:25,760 --> 00:15:31,080
But if it fixes a bottleneck that's costing us far more in hidden losses, then it's worth

232
00:15:31,080 --> 00:15:32,580
considering.

233
00:15:32,580 --> 00:15:33,580
Exactly.

234
00:15:33,580 --> 00:15:37,960
The cost of inefficiency is greater than the cost of improvement.

235
00:15:37,960 --> 00:15:38,960
Well said.

236
00:15:38,960 --> 00:15:43,040
Now, let's move on to workload and morale.

237
00:15:43,040 --> 00:15:45,380
We've seen early signs of burnout.

238
00:15:45,380 --> 00:15:51,040
People aren't complaining loudly, but subtle comments and behavior tell a story.

239
00:15:51,040 --> 00:15:52,660
Have you noticed the same?

240
00:15:52,660 --> 00:15:53,840
Yes.

241
00:15:53,840 --> 00:15:57,480
Many team members feel they're constantly firefighting.

242
00:15:57,480 --> 00:15:59,960
And firefighting creates fatigue.

243
00:15:59,960 --> 00:16:03,820
They want clarity, stability, and direction.

244
00:16:03,820 --> 00:16:05,640
Then we need to give them that.

245
00:16:05,640 --> 00:16:08,800
We can't keep pushing without supporting them.

246
00:16:08,800 --> 00:16:09,800
Agreed.

247
00:16:09,800 --> 00:16:12,040
We need structural clarity.

248
00:16:12,040 --> 00:16:13,160
What's urgent?

249
00:16:13,160 --> 00:16:14,400
What's important?

250
00:16:14,400 --> 00:16:17,230
It's optional and what can wait.

251
00:16:17,230 --> 00:16:22,790
Right now everything feels urgent to them because priorities change too often.

252
00:16:22,790 --> 00:16:25,350
We need a priority architecture.

253
00:16:25,350 --> 00:16:26,750
Exactly.

254
00:16:26,750 --> 00:16:29,790
Let's talk about communication breakdowns.

255
00:16:29,790 --> 00:16:31,950
Where are the biggest gaps?

256
00:16:31,950 --> 00:16:33,450
Three areas.

257
00:16:33,450 --> 00:16:39,190
First, interdepartmental communication is inconsistent, especially between sales and

258
00:16:39,190 --> 00:16:40,190
operations.

259
00:16:40,190 --> 00:16:45,470
Second, mid-level managers often receive updates, but not context.

260
00:16:45,470 --> 00:16:51,790
Third, information flows upward too slowly and downward without clarity.

261
00:16:51,790 --> 00:16:58,750
So basically, we need to redesign how communication happens, not just what is communicated.

262
00:16:58,750 --> 00:17:03,130
Yes, we need real-time channels, not email threads.

263
00:17:03,130 --> 00:17:06,890
We need clear action owners, not vague instructions.

264
00:17:06,890 --> 00:17:12,430
And we need coordination meetings with structured agendas, not informal chats.

265
00:17:12,430 --> 00:17:13,430
Make sense.

266
00:17:13,430 --> 00:17:16,670
Now let's shift to execution discipline.

267
00:17:16,670 --> 00:17:20,150
What slows execution the most?

268
00:17:20,150 --> 00:17:22,230
Unclear responsibilities.

269
00:17:22,230 --> 00:17:26,510
Sometimes two departments think the other one is handling something, and sometimes nobody's

270
00:17:26,510 --> 00:17:28,110
handling it.

271
00:17:28,110 --> 00:17:30,310
That's when delays happen.

272
00:17:30,310 --> 00:17:32,590
Classic accountability gap.

273
00:17:32,590 --> 00:17:33,790
Exactly.

274
00:17:33,790 --> 00:17:36,710
Then we need a clear ownership matrix.

275
00:17:36,710 --> 00:17:41,790
Every task must have one owner, not two, not zero.

276
00:17:41,790 --> 00:17:42,790
Agreed.

277
00:17:42,790 --> 00:17:46,270
Let's pivot to financial performance.

278
00:17:46,270 --> 00:17:49,670
What's your take on the cost structure right now?

279
00:17:49,670 --> 00:17:51,200
Bloated.

280
00:17:51,200 --> 00:17:55,360
Not disastrously, but enough to cut into profitability.

281
00:17:55,360 --> 00:17:59,560
We're spending too much on emergency logistics and repairs.

282
00:17:59,560 --> 00:18:03,800
Unplanned costs always exceed planned costs.

283
00:18:03,800 --> 00:18:08,240
Which tells us we need better anticipation, not more budget.

284
00:18:08,240 --> 00:18:09,880
Exactly.

285
00:18:09,880 --> 00:18:12,600
Predictability is cheaper than improvising.

286
00:18:12,600 --> 00:18:14,000
Well said.

287
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,720
Now what about inventory balance?

288
00:18:16,720 --> 00:18:19,840
Are we holding too much or too little?

289
00:18:19,840 --> 00:18:21,240
A mix.

290
00:18:21,240 --> 00:18:24,940
Some skews are overstocked, others are understocked.

291
00:18:24,940 --> 00:18:30,960
The imbalance comes from inconsistent demand signals and slow adjustment cycles.

292
00:18:30,960 --> 00:18:34,120
So we need dynamic inventory tuning.

293
00:18:34,120 --> 00:18:35,280
Yes.

294
00:18:35,280 --> 00:18:37,800
And cross-team alignment.

295
00:18:37,800 --> 00:18:40,160
Let's talk about the leadership team.

296
00:18:40,160 --> 00:18:42,400
Do you feel they're aligned?

297
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:46,520
Aligned in intent, misaligned in execution.

298
00:18:46,520 --> 00:18:52,020
Everyone wants improvement, but not everyone understands the same priorities.

299
00:18:52,020 --> 00:18:57,280
Some focus on short-term wins, others focus on long-term stability.

300
00:18:57,280 --> 00:18:59,120
That's dangerous.

301
00:18:59,120 --> 00:19:07,280
Must be unified. Yes. Otherwise we're rumbling in different directions. What's causing the misalignment?

302
00:19:07,280 --> 00:19:13,600
Lack of shared visibility. Some leaders see only their department, not the whole system.

303
00:19:13,600 --> 00:19:22,080
So they optimize locally instead of systemically. Exactly. Now about the escalation that happened

304
00:19:22,080 --> 00:19:28,160
last week, the one with the delayed shipment. What went wrong? A sequence of avoidable

305
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:34,200
mistakes. Procurement assumed logistics had coordinated documentation. Logistics

306
00:19:34,200 --> 00:19:39,680
assumed production sent the batch details. Production assumed sales requested

307
00:19:39,680 --> 00:19:45,320
the shipping window. So three assumptions collided and nobody owned the final

308
00:19:45,320 --> 00:19:51,560
check. And the customer paid the price. Yes, and they weren't happy.

309
00:19:51,560 --> 00:19:59,960
understandably. So we need a verification step, a final checkpoint before orders leave the facility.

310
00:20:00,600 --> 00:20:04,680
Exactly. A single point of verification. No assumptions.

311
00:20:05,320 --> 00:20:11,640
Let's move toward future direction. What do you see as the biggest risk in the next six months?

312
00:20:12,360 --> 00:20:18,440
If we don't fix internal alignment, the organization will feel increasingly reactive.

313
00:20:18,440 --> 00:20:22,830
that leads to burnout, turnover, and declining quality.

314
00:20:22,830 --> 00:20:25,790
The risk isn't external, it's internal.

315
00:20:25,790 --> 00:20:29,310
Agreed. And the biggest opportunity?

316
00:20:29,310 --> 00:20:32,910
If we strengthen our structure and processes now,

317
00:20:32,910 --> 00:20:37,230
we can scale efficiently later. That's our competitive advantage.

318
00:20:37,230 --> 00:20:42,110
Absolutely. Discipline first, growth second.

319
00:20:42,110 --> 00:20:47,310
We've covered everything from operations to morale to finance to alignment.

320
00:20:47,310 --> 00:20:51,390
Let's conclude with next steps. What do you propose?

321
00:20:51,390 --> 00:21:01,800
I propose eight actions. Weekly rolling forecast updates, clear ownership matrix for all cross-functional tasks,

322
00:21:01,800 --> 00:21:07,640
real-time communication channel for operational issues, preventative maintenance program and

323
00:21:07,640 --> 00:21:15,100
CAPEX proposal, rebalanced workload and morale initiatives, leadership alignment sessions

324
00:21:15,100 --> 00:21:22,870
with transparent priorities, verification checkpoint for outgoing shipments, and monthly

325
00:21:22,870 --> 00:21:28,050
performance review cycle focused on process, not just results.

326
00:21:28,050 --> 00:21:32,290
That's comprehensive, and exactly what we need.

327
00:21:32,290 --> 00:21:37,210
I'll prepare the documentation, communicate with team leads, and initiate the first

328
00:21:37,210 --> 00:21:38,210
steps.

329
00:21:38,210 --> 00:21:39,210
Perfect.

330
00:21:39,210 --> 00:21:42,830
I feel much clearer after this conversation.

331
00:21:42,830 --> 00:21:43,990
Same here.

332
00:21:43,990 --> 00:21:48,830
When we talk honestly and practically, solutions emerge naturally.

333
00:21:48,830 --> 00:21:50,230
Then let's move forward.

334
00:21:50,230 --> 00:21:53,630
Stronger, clearer, and aligned.

335
00:21:53,630 --> 00:21:54,630
Agreed.

336
00:21:54,630 --> 00:21:57,080
Let's get to work.

337
00:21:57,080 --> 00:22:00,360
Thank you for joining this meeting on short notice.

338
00:22:00,360 --> 00:22:03,760
We have several critical agenda points today.

339
00:22:03,760 --> 00:22:08,960
But more importantly, we need clarity on the direction the corporation is taking

340
00:22:08,960 --> 00:22:11,920
over the next five to seven years.

341
00:22:11,920 --> 00:22:18,560
I believe we've reached a point where incremental adjustments are no longer sufficient.

342
00:22:18,560 --> 00:22:22,360
We need a strategic recalibration.

343
00:22:22,360 --> 00:22:23,360
I agree.

344
00:22:23,360 --> 00:22:30,200
The environment around us is changing faster than our internal processes can adapt.

345
00:22:30,200 --> 00:22:33,480
Competition is becoming more sophisticated.

346
00:22:33,480 --> 00:22:35,520
Regulations are evolving.

347
00:22:35,520 --> 00:22:38,320
Consumer expectations are rising.

348
00:22:38,320 --> 00:22:41,520
And emerging markets are behaving unpredictably.

349
00:22:41,520 --> 00:22:49,520
If we don't reposition ourselves decisively, we risk falling behind, even with our scale advantage.

350
00:22:49,520 --> 00:22:55,940
Exactly. Scale alone isn't the safety net it was even five years ago.

351
00:22:55,940 --> 00:23:01,940
What used to be our greatest competitive advantage, distribution reach, manufacturing footprint,

352
00:23:01,940 --> 00:23:07,940
and global brand presence is now being challenged by players who are far more agile.

353
00:23:07,940 --> 00:23:12,920
They move rapidly, test aggressively, and pivot immediately.

354
00:23:12,920 --> 00:23:18,060
Our cycles, by comparison, still feel sluggish.

355
00:23:18,060 --> 00:23:24,700
The irony is that we've invested heavily in technology, process improvement, and global

356
00:23:24,700 --> 00:23:28,780
alignment initiatives, yet the agility gap persists.

357
00:23:28,780 --> 00:23:32,180
I think part of the issue is structural.

358
00:23:32,180 --> 00:23:39,260
We're operating with legacy layers that were designed for stability, not speed.

359
00:23:39,260 --> 00:23:46,300
Stability served us well for decades, but today the market demands something different.

360
00:23:46,300 --> 00:23:52,540
Which brings us to the core question, what strategic identity should we prioritize?

361
00:23:52,540 --> 00:23:59,140
Are we aiming to be an innovation leader, a customer experience benchmark, a cost efficiency

362
00:23:59,140 --> 00:24:08,160
powerhouse, or a diversified global portfolio that balances stability with selective innovation.

363
00:24:08,160 --> 00:24:12,000
My view is that we need a hybrid approach.

364
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:18,340
Innovation leadership in our primary segments, operational excellence in the legacy segments,

365
00:24:18,340 --> 00:24:23,000
and customer-centric transformation across every market.

366
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:29,160
But executing a hybrid model requires an underlying operating philosophy we currently

367
00:24:29,160 --> 00:24:37,510
lack. We need clarity that cascades down each market, each division, each function.

368
00:24:37,510 --> 00:24:42,730
Agreed. And our regional teams have been asking for this. They keep telling us that

369
00:24:42,730 --> 00:24:49,630
corporate strategy feels too conceptual. They want concrete frameworks, not just directional

370
00:24:49,630 --> 00:24:54,620
aspirations. They need actionable pathways.

371
00:24:54,620 --> 00:24:59,620
Then perhaps we should begin by redefining our core strategic pillars for the next

372
00:24:59,620 --> 00:25:04,620
We can refine them later, but we need a baseline to align on.

373
00:25:04,620 --> 00:25:06,620
Let's start with the key question.

374
00:25:06,620 --> 00:25:10,620
What is the overarching ambition for the corporation?

375
00:25:10,620 --> 00:25:14,010
I would articulate it like this.

376
00:25:14,010 --> 00:25:21,010
To become the world's most adaptive, customer-driven, multinational corporation,

377
00:25:21,010 --> 00:25:26,010
powered by scalable innovation and operational excellence,

378
00:25:26,010 --> 00:25:33,550
excellence. It captures agility, customer centricity, and the balance between innovation

379
00:25:33,550 --> 00:25:36,140
and efficiency.

380
00:25:36,140 --> 00:25:43,140
That phrasing is strong. It reflects ambition, but also acknowledges the foundational strengths

381
00:25:43,140 --> 00:25:50,220
we already possess. But ambition alone is insufficient. We must break it down into

382
00:25:50,220 --> 00:25:57,420
operationalizable pillars. I propose four strategic pillars, customer-driven growth,

383
00:25:58,140 --> 00:26:04,140
operational excellence and scalability, innovation leadership and digital reinvention,

384
00:26:04,860 --> 00:26:08,860
and organizational agility and talent transformation.

385
00:26:10,060 --> 00:26:17,660
Those pillars seem comprehensive. Let's unpack each one. Starting with customer-driven growth,

386
00:26:17,660 --> 00:26:25,180
What does that mean for a multinational operating in over 45 countries with varied maturity levels?

387
00:26:26,340 --> 00:26:33,780
It means shifting from a product-centric mindset to a solution-centric mindset. Historically,

388
00:26:33,780 --> 00:26:40,820
we have exported global products into local markets. Going forward, we need regionalized

389
00:26:40,820 --> 00:26:47,660
innovation, segment-specific value propositions, and customer journey optimization tailored

390
00:26:47,660 --> 00:26:50,250
to local behaviors.

391
00:26:50,250 --> 00:26:56,650
So essentially, empowerment of regional teams with structured guardrails from corporate

392
00:26:56,650 --> 00:27:04,210
because full autonomy risks fragmentation while centralization limits relevance.

393
00:27:04,210 --> 00:27:05,650
Exactly.

394
00:27:05,650 --> 00:27:08,490
What we need is guided autonomy.

395
00:27:08,490 --> 00:27:14,810
The framework sets the direction, but markets choose the execution pathway based on customer

396
00:27:14,810 --> 00:27:18,540
insights and ecosystem dynamics.

397
00:27:18,540 --> 00:27:21,420
That brings us to the challenge of data.

398
00:27:21,420 --> 00:27:26,800
Our customer analytics are fragmented, inconsistent, and often delayed.

399
00:27:26,800 --> 00:27:32,620
If we want to be customer-driven, we need an integrated data platform that unifies

400
00:27:32,620 --> 00:27:40,060
purchase behavior, channel preference, journey moments, loyalty triggers, and satisfaction metrics.

401
00:27:41,460 --> 00:27:48,820
Yes, but integration alone won't solve the problem. We need analytical capability at the local level.

402
00:27:49,460 --> 00:27:56,020
Many of our regional teams simply don't have the talent or tools to extract actionable insights.

403
00:27:56,580 --> 00:28:00,180
Without capability building, the data will just sit there.

404
00:28:00,180 --> 00:28:07,120
Capability building has to be a strategic mandate, not an optional initiative, which

405
00:28:07,120 --> 00:28:13,800
ties directly into your fourth pillar, organizational agility and talent transformation.

406
00:28:13,800 --> 00:28:19,680
Our leadership pipeline, for example, still favors experience over adaptability.

407
00:28:19,680 --> 00:28:22,730
That model is outdated.

408
00:28:22,730 --> 00:28:24,690
Precisely.

409
00:28:24,690 --> 00:28:29,570
Experience is valuable, but adaptability is mission critical.

410
00:28:29,570 --> 00:28:35,850
The leaders of the future are those who can operate in ambiguity, manage diverse stakeholder

411
00:28:35,850 --> 00:28:41,050
groups and make fast decisions without perfect information.

412
00:28:41,050 --> 00:28:46,140
Our current development programs are too linear and slow.

413
00:28:46,140 --> 00:28:51,180
And this raises another concern I want us to address honestly.

414
00:28:51,180 --> 00:28:57,540
Are we structured in a way that allows talent to excel or do our hierarchical layers

415
00:28:57,540 --> 00:29:00,160
suppress initiative?

416
00:29:00,160 --> 00:29:07,420
If we're being candid, yes, our structure is still too hierarchical.

417
00:29:07,420 --> 00:29:10,160
Decision-making layers slow us down.

418
00:29:10,160 --> 00:29:16,260
Even simple approvals sometimes move through three or four levels unnecessarily.

419
00:29:16,260 --> 00:29:24,270
It creates frustration and reduces accountability because decisions become distributed and diluted.

420
00:29:24,270 --> 00:29:29,690
Then one of our first operational decisions should be simplification, a reduction in

421
00:29:29,690 --> 00:29:37,100
bureaucratic layers, clearer decision rights, and more empowered cross-functional units.

422
00:29:37,100 --> 00:29:42,940
I support that direction. But we'll need a change management roadmap. Structural changes

423
00:29:42,940 --> 00:29:49,980
without behavioral alignment will create confusion. People need clarity on what empowerment means

424
00:29:49,980 --> 00:29:57,640
and what responsibilities accompany it. True. Empowerment isn't about relinquishing control.

425
00:29:57,640 --> 00:30:03,800
it's about assigning ownership with accountability. That distinction must be communicated.

426
00:30:05,150 --> 00:30:11,790
Let's move to the second pillar, operational excellence and scalability. Our operational

427
00:30:11,790 --> 00:30:19,710
footprint spans manufacturing, logistics, procurement, quality, and supply chain. Yet our

428
00:30:19,710 --> 00:30:26,030
global integration is inconsistent. Some regions operate with world-class efficiency,

429
00:30:26,030 --> 00:30:29,030
other's lags significantly.

430
00:30:29,030 --> 00:30:33,380
That inconsistency is costing us millions annually.

431
00:30:33,380 --> 00:30:37,380
We need a unified operational excellence model,

432
00:30:37,380 --> 00:30:41,380
a standardized playbook that outlines best practices

433
00:30:41,380 --> 00:30:44,380
for manufacturing, digital supply chain,

434
00:30:44,380 --> 00:30:49,380
automation, predictive analytics, and ESG compliance.

435
00:30:49,380 --> 00:30:53,760
Right now our operations function behaves more like

436
00:30:53,760 --> 00:30:59,320
a collection of regional clusters, rather than a global ecosystem.

437
00:30:59,320 --> 00:31:07,040
We need to build a global center of excellence that drives continuous improvement across all markets.

438
00:31:07,040 --> 00:31:12,460
And we need tighter alignment between supply planning and demand forecasting.

439
00:31:12,460 --> 00:31:16,720
Demand forecasting accuracy is one of our ongoing weaknesses.

440
00:31:16,720 --> 00:31:22,000
It results in either excess inventory or stockouts in key markets.

441
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:24,280
Both are costly.

442
00:31:24,280 --> 00:31:30,120
Demand forecasting will only improve when the commercial teams and operations function

443
00:31:30,120 --> 00:31:37,000
communicate more transparently. Right now, insights flow in one direction. Commercial pushes

444
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:44,120
numbers upward, but operations rarely pushes back with scenario evaluations or risk assessments.

445
00:31:45,380 --> 00:31:52,260
So we need integrated forecasting teams, a blend of commercial, finance, and supply chain experts

446
00:31:52,260 --> 00:31:54,660
working together with shared KPIs.

447
00:31:55,680 --> 00:31:59,040
Yes. Shared KPIs are essential.

448
00:31:59,520 --> 00:32:03,920
Curseably, each function optimizes for its own success metrics,

449
00:32:04,120 --> 00:32:08,320
which sometimes conflict with the corporation's overall objectives.

450
00:32:08,760 --> 00:32:12,080
We need a synchronized KPI architecture.

451
00:32:13,010 --> 00:32:15,530
Let's move on to the third pillar,

452
00:32:15,770 --> 00:32:19,130
innovation, leadership and digital reinvention.

453
00:32:19,130 --> 00:32:27,130
We've invested heavily in digital, yet our innovation speed still trails smaller competitors, why?

454
00:32:27,130 --> 00:32:35,610
Because our digital investments were driven by technology, not by customer journeys or strategic objectives.

455
00:32:35,610 --> 00:32:40,610
We have tools, but we don't have integrated digital ecosystems.

456
00:32:40,610 --> 00:32:47,840
Exactly. We must shift from technology adoption to capability transformation.

457
00:32:47,840 --> 00:32:51,220
Innovation should be driven by unmet co…

458
00:32:51,220 --> 00:32:56,240
Future skills certification is essential, but it must be meaningful.

459
00:32:56,240 --> 00:33:00,840
Employees should see direct correlation between acquiring new skills and opportunities for

460
00:33:00,840 --> 00:33:03,760
career progression or project ownership.

461
00:33:03,760 --> 00:33:08,160
Exactly, and it should be integrated into our succession planning.

462
00:33:08,160 --> 00:33:11,200
We can't just develop talent for the sake of development.

463
00:33:11,200 --> 00:33:15,680
We need a clear path from capability to leadership roles.

464
00:33:15,680 --> 00:33:19,200
That also means rethinking our global talent mobility.

465
00:33:19,200 --> 00:33:24,440
High potential employees should rotate across regions to gain exposure to different market

466
00:33:24,440 --> 00:33:27,680
dynamics, cultures, and operational models.

467
00:33:27,680 --> 00:33:31,760
Yes, rotation builds both perspective and network.

468
00:33:31,760 --> 00:33:34,800
But we also need to avoid over-rotation.

469
00:33:34,800 --> 00:33:40,120
The goal is depth in regional understanding while maintaining global exposure.

470
00:33:40,120 --> 00:33:41,120
Agreed.

471
00:33:41,120 --> 00:33:45,960
We should map career journeys with a mix of lateral and vertical moves, ensuring each

472
00:33:45,960 --> 00:33:50,760
rotation strengthens both functional and strategic capabilities.

473
00:33:50,760 --> 00:33:55,760
On a related note, we need to discuss succession planning in executive roles.

474
00:33:55,760 --> 00:33:58,120
Our current approach is reactive.

475
00:33:58,120 --> 00:34:04,720
We identify successors late and sometimes promote based on tenure rather than capability.

476
00:34:04,720 --> 00:34:06,680
That's a major risk.

477
00:34:06,680 --> 00:34:12,080
succession should be a living plan, updated quarterly, aligned with the strategic pillars

478
00:34:12,080 --> 00:34:18,200
and tested against multiple scenarios. Scenario testing is key. We need simulations

479
00:34:18,200 --> 00:34:25,120
that anticipate business disruptions, economic shocks, regulatory changes, competitive pressures,

480
00:34:25,120 --> 00:34:31,240
and evaluate how potential leaders respond. And these simulations should not be confined

481
00:34:31,240 --> 00:34:36,520
to board-level exercises. They should cascade down to functional and regional leaders.

482
00:34:36,520 --> 00:34:41,060
We want a culture where scenario thinking is standard practice.

483
00:34:41,060 --> 00:34:47,500
Moving to the topic of digital reinvention, how do you see AI and advanced analytics transforming

484
00:34:47,500 --> 00:34:50,100
our decision-making processes?

485
00:34:50,100 --> 00:34:51,700
AI has two main roles.

486
00:34:51,700 --> 00:34:57,740
First, it enhances operational efficiency by predicting trends, optimizing supply chains

487
00:34:57,740 --> 00:35:00,180
and automating repetitive tasks.

488
00:35:00,180 --> 00:35:05,580
Second, it empowers decision-making with predictive insights, but we must avoid treating

489
00:35:05,580 --> 00:35:07,660
AI as infallible.

490
00:35:07,660 --> 00:35:08,660
Agreed.

491
00:35:08,660 --> 00:35:13,260
AI is an augmentation tool, not a replacement for judgment.

492
00:35:13,260 --> 00:35:19,340
Leaders still need to contextualize insights, apply experience, and evaluate risk.

493
00:35:19,340 --> 00:35:21,300
That's why governance is crucial.

494
00:35:21,300 --> 00:35:26,580
We should establish a decision framework where AI insights are mandatory inputs but

495
00:35:26,580 --> 00:35:28,580
not sole determinants.

496
00:35:28,580 --> 00:35:31,420
And this ties into transparency.

497
00:35:31,420 --> 00:35:35,860
must understand how AI conclusions are derived.

498
00:35:35,860 --> 00:35:38,500
Explainable AI is no longer optional.

499
00:35:38,500 --> 00:35:40,620
It's a trust requirement.

500
00:35:40,620 --> 00:35:42,020
Exactly.

501
00:35:42,020 --> 00:35:46,740
Explainable AI ensures that business decisions can be audited and defended both internally

502
00:35:46,740 --> 00:35:50,990
and externally, especially in regulated markets.

503
00:35:50,990 --> 00:35:52,990
Let's talk about market expansion.

504
00:35:52,990 --> 00:35:55,990
We've historically relied on organic growth.

505
00:35:55,990 --> 00:36:01,070
But should we consider more aggressive M&A activity in strategic segments?

506
00:36:01,070 --> 00:36:03,070
Possibly, but with discipline.

507
00:36:03,070 --> 00:36:06,710
Acquisitions should reinforce our core strategic pillars,

508
00:36:06,710 --> 00:36:08,510
not distract from them.

509
00:36:08,510 --> 00:36:11,630
Every potential acquisition needs a clear rationale.

510
00:36:11,630 --> 00:36:14,590
Growth acceleration, capability acquisition,

511
00:36:14,590 --> 00:36:16,470
or market entry.

512
00:36:16,470 --> 00:36:19,870
And we must evaluate integration risk upfront.

513
00:36:19,870 --> 00:36:23,310
Poorly integrated acquisitions have historically eroded

514
00:36:23,310 --> 00:36:25,790
value rather than created it.

515
00:36:25,790 --> 00:36:29,190
Integration planning must begin before the deal closes.

516
00:36:29,190 --> 00:36:32,150
Leadership alignment, systems compatibility,

517
00:36:32,150 --> 00:36:35,310
cultural assessment, and communication strategy

518
00:36:35,310 --> 00:36:38,110
all need to be in place early.

519
00:36:38,110 --> 00:36:39,830
On the topic of culture,

520
00:36:39,830 --> 00:36:43,110
I want to address the innovation mindset.

521
00:36:43,110 --> 00:36:46,850
Many regions still prioritize operational adherence

522
00:36:46,850 --> 00:36:48,950
over creative problem solving.

523
00:36:48,950 --> 00:36:50,910
How do we shift this mindset

524
00:36:50,910 --> 00:36:53,670
without undermining accountability?

525
00:36:53,670 --> 00:36:55,830
By embedding innovation metrics

526
00:36:55,830 --> 00:36:58,390
alongside operational KPIs.

527
00:36:58,390 --> 00:37:04,770
For instance, measure not only efficiency, but also idea generation, implementation speed,

528
00:37:04,770 --> 00:37:07,250
and cross-functional collaboration.

529
00:37:07,250 --> 00:37:10,890
And incentivize calculated risk-taking.

530
00:37:10,890 --> 00:37:13,490
Employees should feel safe to experiment.

531
00:37:13,490 --> 00:37:18,410
Mistakes should be viewed as learning opportunities, not failures.

532
00:37:18,410 --> 00:37:20,490
Leadership behavior is critical here.

533
00:37:20,490 --> 00:37:25,490
If leaders visibly reward experimentation and curiosity, the culture shift will follow

534
00:37:25,490 --> 00:37:27,490
more naturally.

535
00:37:27,490 --> 00:37:30,210
Another aspect is external partnerships.

536
00:37:30,210 --> 00:37:35,850
We've been too cautious in collaborating with startups, technology vendors, and research

537
00:37:35,850 --> 00:37:37,410
institutions.

538
00:37:37,410 --> 00:37:40,490
Strategic alliances could accelerate innovation.

539
00:37:40,490 --> 00:37:42,330
Absolutely.

540
00:37:42,330 --> 00:37:47,810
Co-creation ecosystems allow us to test concepts rapidly, leverage external expertise, and

541
00:37:47,810 --> 00:37:49,010
share risk.

542
00:37:49,010 --> 00:37:55,010
But we need structured processes to evaluate, onboard, and manage partners effectively.

543
00:37:55,010 --> 00:37:56,890
Which leads us to governance.

544
00:37:56,890 --> 00:37:59,830
We can't have innovation run ad hoc.

545
00:37:59,830 --> 00:38:06,150
A formal innovation governance council could oversee pipeline prioritization, budget allocation,

546
00:38:06,150 --> 00:38:07,870
and outcome tracking.

547
00:38:07,870 --> 00:38:08,870
Agreed.

548
00:38:08,870 --> 00:38:14,390
It should include representation from strategy, operations, finance, and regional leaders.

549
00:38:14,390 --> 00:38:20,440
The council ensures alignment, avoids duplication, and validates business impact.

550
00:38:20,440 --> 00:38:25,600
Now regarding operational scalability, I've noticed supply chain resilience is still

551
00:38:25,600 --> 00:38:31,200
inconsistent across regions. How can we create a unified approach?

552
00:38:31,200 --> 00:38:36,620
We need a global operations blueprint that standardizes processes while allowing regional

553
00:38:36,620 --> 00:38:42,940
adaptation. Automation, predictive inventory, and integrated logistics platforms are central

554
00:38:42,940 --> 00:38:44,440
to this.

555
00:38:44,440 --> 00:38:49,880
And we must build contingency planning. Each region should maintain a tiered risk map

556
00:38:49,880 --> 00:38:55,000
with mitigation protocols for disruptions from regulatory shifts to natural disasters.

557
00:38:55,720 --> 00:39:02,040
Technology can support this. Advanced analytics can simulate disruptions and optimize responses.

558
00:39:02,040 --> 00:39:07,000
But humans must interpret and execute plans. Pure automation is insufficient.

559
00:39:07,890 --> 00:39:11,890
Let's discuss environmental, social, and governance objectives.

560
00:39:11,890 --> 00:39:16,210
Investors are scrutinizing ESG performance more than ever.

561
00:39:16,210 --> 00:39:19,650
How do we integrate ESG into our operational DNA?

562
00:39:20,400 --> 00:39:24,160
ESG needs to be operationalized, not just reported.

563
00:39:24,160 --> 00:39:28,240
For instance, sustainable sourcing, carbon neutral manufacturing,

564
00:39:28,240 --> 00:39:34,080
diversity in leadership, and ethical supply chain practices should be measurable KPIs.

565
00:39:34,080 --> 00:39:36,400
And tied to performance incentives.

566
00:39:36,400 --> 00:39:42,400
If leaders' bonuses depend partially on ESG outcomes, the organization takes it seriously.

567
00:39:42,400 --> 00:39:49,280
Indeed, ESG integration also strengthens our brand perception in global markets and mitigates

568
00:39:49,280 --> 00:39:52,560
regulatory and reputational risk.

569
00:39:52,560 --> 00:39:55,080
Switching to digital transformation.

570
00:39:55,080 --> 00:40:01,240
We've implemented multiple systems across regions, ERP, CRM, analytics.

571
00:40:01,240 --> 00:40:03,080
But adoption varies.

572
00:40:03,080 --> 00:40:07,360
How do we standardize usage without imposing rigidity?

573
00:40:07,360 --> 00:40:11,560
We should focus on capability enablement, not tool enforcement.

574
00:40:11,560 --> 00:40:16,240
Training, coaching, and peer-led knowledge sharing can drive adoption more effectively

575
00:40:16,240 --> 00:40:18,520
than mandates alone.

576
00:40:18,520 --> 00:40:24,720
And we need executive sponsorship in each region to reinforce accountability.

577
00:40:24,720 --> 00:40:27,760
Tools alone don't change behavior.

578
00:40:27,760 --> 00:40:29,200
Leadership does.

579
00:40:29,200 --> 00:40:30,200
Agreed.

580
00:40:30,200 --> 00:40:31,920
Let's move to financial strategy.

581
00:40:31,920 --> 00:40:36,000
Our revenue mix across segments shows stagnation in certain regions.

582
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:39,110
How do we prioritize investment?

583
00:40:39,110 --> 00:40:44,050
Resource allocation should reflect both market potential and strategic importance.

584
00:40:44,050 --> 00:40:48,990
High growth segments deserve capital infusion for expansion and innovation.

585
00:40:48,990 --> 00:40:53,830
Low growth segments require cost discipline and efficiency programs.

586
00:40:53,830 --> 00:40:58,630
But we must also be careful not to starve regions with latent potential.

587
00:40:58,630 --> 00:41:04,510
Early stage investments in emerging markets may not show immediate ROI but can yield long-term

588
00:41:04,510 --> 00:41:10,830
competitive advantage? Correct. A balanced portfolio approach is necessary. Short-term

589
00:41:10,830 --> 00:41:16,630
profitability for mature markets, long-term growth for emerging markets, and financial

590
00:41:16,630 --> 00:41:22,630
performance must be linked to operational KPIs. For example, supply chain efficiency,

591
00:41:22,630 --> 00:41:27,510
customer acquisition costs, and innovation pipeline velocity should influence budget

592
00:41:27,510 --> 00:41:33,600
allocation. Talent costs are also a factor. Hiring globally competitive skill sets,

593
00:41:33,600 --> 00:41:37,380
particularly in digital and analytics, is expensive.

594
00:41:37,380 --> 00:41:40,720
We need a holistic cost to impact model.

595
00:41:40,720 --> 00:41:43,680
Speaking of talent, I've noticed retention risks

596
00:41:43,680 --> 00:41:45,480
in high potential cohorts.

597
00:41:45,480 --> 00:41:47,280
Our current engagement programs

598
00:41:47,280 --> 00:41:50,520
don't sufficiently differentiate top performers.

599
00:41:50,520 --> 00:41:53,320
Then we need targeted retention strategies,

600
00:41:53,320 --> 00:41:56,280
career acceleration, skill development,

601
00:41:56,280 --> 00:42:00,720
global exposure, and meaningful project ownership.

602
00:42:00,720 --> 00:42:03,880
and competitive rewards, but not purely monetary.

603
00:42:03,880 --> 00:42:07,120
Recognition, autonomy, and purpose-driven projects

604
00:42:07,120 --> 00:42:10,180
can be more motivating for high performers.

605
00:42:10,180 --> 00:42:12,140
Moving to risk management.

606
00:42:12,140 --> 00:42:15,300
Geopolitical shifts and supply chain vulnerabilities

607
00:42:15,300 --> 00:42:17,540
are increasingly material.

608
00:42:17,540 --> 00:42:19,860
How do we future-proof?

609
00:42:19,860 --> 00:42:23,020
Risk mapping must be continuous and predictive.

610
00:42:23,020 --> 00:42:24,660
Advanced scenario planning,

611
00:42:24,660 --> 00:42:26,380
geopolitical intelligence,

612
00:42:26,380 --> 00:42:30,100
and supply chain diversification are key levers.

613
00:42:30,100 --> 00:42:32,540
Cyber security is also critical.

614
00:42:32,540 --> 00:42:35,620
Digital integration increases exposure.

615
00:42:35,620 --> 00:42:39,140
Are we confident our systems are resilient enough?

616
00:42:39,140 --> 00:42:42,540
We've strengthened infrastructure, but security is dynamic.

617
00:42:42,540 --> 00:42:46,140
Regular penetration testing, AI-driven threat detection,

618
00:42:46,140 --> 00:42:48,060
and employee awareness programs

619
00:42:48,060 --> 00:42:50,960
must remain ongoing priorities.

620
00:42:50,960 --> 00:42:52,720
Returning to market strategy,

621
00:42:52,720 --> 00:42:56,180
I'd like to explore portfolio rationalization.

622
00:42:56,180 --> 00:42:59,120
Are there non-core assets we should divest

623
00:42:59,120 --> 00:43:01,410
to fund strategic growth?

624
00:43:01,410 --> 00:43:02,290
Certainly.

625
00:43:02,290 --> 00:43:05,090
Non-core businesses that deliver low returns

626
00:43:05,090 --> 00:43:07,530
or no longer align with strategic pillars

627
00:43:07,530 --> 00:43:08,970
should be reviewed.

628
00:43:08,970 --> 00:43:11,370
Divestment can free capital and management

629
00:43:11,370 --> 00:43:14,240
attention for higher impact areas.

630
00:43:14,240 --> 00:43:17,560
How do we approach integration across regions?

631
00:43:17,560 --> 00:43:22,040
Some markets adopt best practices quickly, others lag.

632
00:43:22,040 --> 00:43:25,240
Integration requires structured knowledge transfer,

633
00:43:25,240 --> 00:43:27,920
mentoring, and process standardization.

634
00:43:27,920 --> 00:43:33,650
We should document lessons learned in high-performing regions and replicate them globally.

635
00:43:33,650 --> 00:43:40,070
On innovation, I want us to be clear on balancing incremental versus disruptive projects.

636
00:43:40,070 --> 00:43:43,730
Too much focus on the former leads to stagnation.

637
00:43:43,730 --> 00:43:46,570
Too much on the latter risks overextending.

638
00:43:46,570 --> 00:43:53,290
Picking up from culture, we need to ensure our values are embedded in daily operations,

639
00:43:53,290 --> 00:43:55,810
not just statements on the intranet.

640
00:43:55,810 --> 00:43:59,110
How do we operationalize this?

641
00:43:59,110 --> 00:44:00,810
Metrics are essential.

642
00:44:00,810 --> 00:44:06,770
We can measure collaboration through cross-functional projects, innovation through idea submission

643
00:44:06,770 --> 00:44:12,030
rates and execution, and accountability through milestone adherence.

644
00:44:12,030 --> 00:44:16,030
And leadership visibility reinforces these metrics.

645
00:44:16,030 --> 00:44:23,070
When senior leaders actively model the behaviors, it cascades throughout the organization.

646
00:44:23,070 --> 00:44:28,490
On the topic of transformation, digital adoption remains uneven.

647
00:44:28,490 --> 00:44:34,370
We should develop a standardized adoption roadmap across regions, incorporating training,

648
00:44:34,370 --> 00:44:37,610
mentoring, and executive sponsorship.

649
00:44:37,610 --> 00:44:38,610
Agreed.

650
00:44:38,610 --> 00:44:41,610
Tools alone aren't sufficient.

651
00:44:41,610 --> 00:44:47,450
Change management programs must accompany system rollouts to embed behaviors.

652
00:44:47,450 --> 00:44:51,010
Let's examine operational efficiency.

653
00:44:51,010 --> 00:44:56,670
The fly chain digitization is progressing, but predictive analytics adoption lags.

654
00:44:56,670 --> 00:44:58,970
How do we accelerate this?

655
00:44:58,970 --> 00:45:07,410
Prioritize high impact nodes first, warehouses, transportation hubs, and inventory heavy regions.

656
00:45:07,410 --> 00:45:14,290
Use predictive models to optimize stock levels, route planning, and demand forecasting.

657
00:45:14,290 --> 00:45:17,250
And then scale to secondary regions.

658
00:45:17,250 --> 00:45:22,370
We should ensure the system is flexible to accommodate local constraints while maintaining

659
00:45:22,370 --> 00:45:24,730
global standards.

660
00:45:24,730 --> 00:45:30,990
Speaking of scaling, talent development must mirror operational expansion.

661
00:45:30,990 --> 00:45:36,630
If we implement advanced analytics in one region, local teams must have the skills

662
00:45:36,630 --> 00:45:38,730
to leverage insights.

663
00:45:38,730 --> 00:45:40,430
Exactly.

664
00:45:40,430 --> 00:45:43,850
That ties into our learning and development strategy.

665
00:45:43,850 --> 00:45:49,890
Mean modules should be role-specific and include simulations for real-world application.

666
00:45:49,890 --> 00:45:54,750
We also need to consider retention of newly trained employees.

667
00:45:54,750 --> 00:45:57,010
High-skill talent is mobile.

668
00:45:57,010 --> 00:46:02,370
Engagement, career pathways, and project ownership are critical.

669
00:46:02,370 --> 00:46:04,330
Switching to innovation.

670
00:46:04,330 --> 00:46:07,330
We discussed portfolio balance.

671
00:46:07,330 --> 00:46:12,890
Incremental innovation is easy to track, but how do we measure exploratory or disruptive

672
00:46:12,890 --> 00:46:21,770
initiatives. Use milestone-based evaluation. Track hypothesis validation, prototype completion,

673
00:46:21,770 --> 00:46:29,210
market testing, and early adoption metrics. ROI is longer term, but structured evaluation

674
00:46:29,210 --> 00:46:36,090
prevents runaway costs. And for disruptive projects, cross-functional mentorship is essential.

675
00:46:36,730 --> 00:46:41,530
Teams often lack diverse perspectives, which can limit creativity or risk assessment.

676
00:46:41,530 --> 00:46:49,210
Partnerships can help too. Collaboration with universities, startups, and research labs accelerates

677
00:46:49,210 --> 00:46:53,250
learning and reduces experimentation risk.

678
00:46:53,250 --> 00:47:00,410
Now, financial oversight. Our global operations involve diverse currencies, tax regimes, and

679
00:47:00,410 --> 00:47:06,090
regulatory frameworks. How do we ensure consistent financial discipline?

680
00:47:06,090 --> 00:47:12,770
Standardized financial KPIs, consolidated reporting and routine audits are crucial.

681
00:47:12,770 --> 00:47:18,970
Transparency and timeliness of reporting allow leadership to intervene before variances

682
00:47:18,970 --> 00:47:21,290
become critical.

683
00:47:21,290 --> 00:47:23,450
Cimmerio modeling also helps.

684
00:47:23,450 --> 00:47:28,890
Running simulations for different macroeconomic conditions, currency fluctuations or supply

685
00:47:28,890 --> 00:47:32,250
shocks enables proactive planning.

686
00:47:32,250 --> 00:47:40,250
and integrating predictive analytics into our financial models improves accuracy and decision confidence.

687
00:47:40,250 --> 00:47:52,250
On ESG again, compliance reporting is necessary but insufficient. How do we make ESG outcomes measurable and tied to performance incentives?

688
00:47:52,250 --> 00:48:00,410
Define specific, quantifiable ESG targets, carbon reduction percentages, supplier diversity

689
00:48:00,410 --> 00:48:06,370
metrics, community investment and safety compliance rates. Link a portion of leadership

690
00:48:06,370 --> 00:48:12,930
and operational bonuses to these outcomes. And reporting must be transparent. Internal

691
00:48:12,930 --> 00:48:18,210
dashboards and external disclosures should reflect progress accurately. This builds

692
00:48:18,210 --> 00:48:20,690
trust with stakeholders and investors.

693
00:48:20,690 --> 00:48:27,330
Speaking of customer experience, AI adoption for personalization is growing, but human

694
00:48:27,330 --> 00:48:29,390
touch remains vital.

695
00:48:29,390 --> 00:48:32,690
How do we balance automation with empathy?

696
00:48:32,690 --> 00:48:35,410
We segment customer interactions.

697
00:48:35,410 --> 00:48:41,570
Routine queries can be automated, complex, high-impact, or emotionally sensitive interactions

698
00:48:41,570 --> 00:48:43,890
require human engagement.

699
00:48:43,890 --> 00:48:50,730
Additionally, AI can provide decision support to human agents, real-time recommendations,

700
00:48:50,730 --> 00:48:55,970
predictive next best actions, and sentiment analysis.

701
00:48:55,970 --> 00:49:01,350
Training customer-facing employees to interpret AI insights effectively ensures they act

702
00:49:01,350 --> 00:49:06,770
with context, enhancing both efficiency and experience.

703
00:49:06,770 --> 00:49:10,050
Data privacy must be embedded too.

704
00:49:10,050 --> 00:49:16,970
Most experiences require trust, which depends on secure handling of customer information.

705
00:49:16,970 --> 00:49:22,690
On global growth strategy, we previously discussed market prioritization.

706
00:49:22,690 --> 00:49:28,330
Should we accelerate entry into emerging economies now or adopt a phased approach?

707
00:49:28,330 --> 00:49:30,770
A phased approach is prudent.

708
00:49:30,770 --> 00:49:36,890
Pilot high potential markets first, validate models, refine go-to-market strategies,

709
00:49:36,890 --> 00:49:39,460
then scale systematically.

710
00:49:39,460 --> 00:49:42,980
But we should maintain visibility on all markets.

711
00:49:42,980 --> 00:49:48,300
Real-time dashboards on market performance, customer adoption, and competitive intelligence

712
00:49:48,300 --> 00:49:50,980
allow agile decision-making.

713
00:49:50,980 --> 00:49:57,300
Speaking of scaling, AI integration into business processes must be continuous.

714
00:49:57,300 --> 00:50:04,460
Predictive analytics, generative AI, and automated reporting can unlock efficiency if aligned

715
00:50:04,460 --> 00:50:06,940
with strategic objectives.

716
00:50:06,940 --> 00:50:10,940
And adoption should be coupled with capability development.

717
00:50:10,940 --> 00:50:16,460
Employees need to understand how to interpret AI outputs and make decisions accordingly.

718
00:50:16,460 --> 00:50:17,980
Agreed.

719
00:50:17,980 --> 00:50:21,260
Leadership must also champion AI usage.

720
00:50:21,260 --> 00:50:26,660
Without executive buy-in, adoption is uneven and inconsistent.

721
00:50:26,660 --> 00:50:32,620
Regarding risk management, geopolitical volatility, cyber threats, and operational disruptions

722
00:50:32,620 --> 00:50:37,900
remain key concerns, how do we institutionalize resilience?

723
00:50:37,900 --> 00:50:43,740
Multilayered risk frameworks are required. Scenario planning, predictive modeling, business

724
00:50:43,740 --> 00:50:50,620
continuity protocols, and regular stress tests build organizational resilience.

725
00:50:50,620 --> 00:50:57,660
Supply chain diversification is central. Overreliance on single suppliers or geographies increases

726
00:50:57,660 --> 00:51:03,660
vulnerability. Cyber security must be proactive. AI-driven threat detection,

727
00:51:03,660 --> 00:51:09,500
regular audits, employee training, and incident response protocols create a

728
00:51:09,500 --> 00:51:14,940
secure environment. Talent risk is another dimension. High potential employees

729
00:51:14,940 --> 00:51:20,140
may leave or skill gaps may emerge in critical areas. Continuous learning

730
00:51:20,140 --> 00:51:25,460
and retention strategies mitigate this. Let's consolidate transformation

731
00:51:25,460 --> 00:51:33,300
initiatives. We have digital adoption, supply chain optimization, talent development, innovation

732
00:51:33,300 --> 00:51:40,500
governance, ESG integration, customer experience enhancement, market expansion, financial

733
00:51:40,500 --> 00:51:47,260
discipline, and risk resilience. Each initiative requires a detailed roadmap,

734
00:51:47,260 --> 00:51:55,660
milestones, responsible owners, budgets, timelines, KPIs, and escalation protocols.

735
00:51:55,660 --> 00:51:58,420
And cross-functional coordination.

736
00:51:58,420 --> 00:52:00,780
Many initiatives intersect.

737
00:52:00,780 --> 00:52:05,900
Alignment is critical to avoid duplication or conflict.

738
00:52:05,900 --> 00:52:07,940
Communication is key too.

739
00:52:07,940 --> 00:52:13,100
Clear articulation of objectives, expected behaviors, and success criteria ensures

740
00:52:13,100 --> 00:52:16,980
alignment across geographies and functions.

741
00:52:16,980 --> 00:52:22,840
Forms should be regular, quarterly strategy reviews, monthly operational reviews, and

742
00:52:22,840 --> 00:52:26,180
weekly check-ins for critical initiatives.

743
00:52:26,180 --> 00:52:28,900
And we need a feedback mechanism.

744
00:52:28,900 --> 00:52:34,420
Employees and managers should have channels to report challenges, insights, and suggestions

745
00:52:34,420 --> 00:52:37,060
to continuously refine the plan.

746
00:52:37,060 --> 00:52:40,260
Metrics should be both leading and lagging.

747
00:52:40,260 --> 00:52:46,940
Leading indicators, adoption rates, training completion, project milestones predict success,

748
00:52:46,940 --> 00:52:53,020
While lagging indicators, financial results, market share, customer satisfaction, validate

749
00:52:53,020 --> 00:52:54,020
outcomes.

750
00:52:54,020 --> 00:52:57,660
Let's address innovation again.

751
00:52:57,660 --> 00:53:02,540
Incremental versus disruptive innovation must be managed as a portfolio.

752
00:53:02,540 --> 00:53:07,700
Resource allocation should be dynamic based on validation, feasibility, and strategic

753
00:53:07,700 --> 00:53:08,940
importance.

754
00:53:08,940 --> 00:53:13,380
Rapid prototyping and iterative testing reduce risk.

755
00:53:13,380 --> 00:53:19,300
All scale pilots allow learning and scalability before full-scale investment.

756
00:53:19,300 --> 00:53:22,900
We should create innovation hubs in key regions.

757
00:53:22,900 --> 00:53:28,580
